    "confirmed": [
      {
        "dimension": "bug",
        "title": "changed_since_last_tag's unscoped commits_since short-circuit marks every package \"changed\" whenever anything in the whole repo committed since its tag",
        "primary_file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/changed.rs",
        "primary_lines": "51-59",
        "other_locations": [
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/status.rs",
            "lines": "86-96"
          }
        ],
        "description": "`changed_since_last_tag` calls `git.commits_since(Some(&qualified), &[])` with an EMPTY pathspec list, then treats any non-empty result as `Ok(true)` (changed) before ever reaching the path-scoped `git diff --quiet <tag> -- <package paths>` check below it. Because the pathspec list is empty, `commits_since` walks and returns every commit anywhere in the repo since the package's last tag -- not commits touching that package. In any monorepo with ongoing activity, this list is virtually always non-empty (some other package always has commits after this one's last release), so the function returns `true` for essentially every package on essentially every call, and the actually-correct, path-scoped `git diff --quiet` fallback a few lines below becomes dead code that only ever fires when the whole repository has had zero commits at all since the tag. This contradicts the algorithm documented in docs/01-spec.md §G.9.3 (`Some(t) -> git diff --quiet <t.name> -- <paths>`, no commits_since short-circuit at all) and defeats the entire purpose of per-package change detection: `status`'s `changed_since_last_tag` field becomes meaningless (near-always true), and §6.3's empty-changeset validation (\"if no package named by `cs` has changed_since_last_tag\") can never trigger its escape-hatch warning because the field is always true. This is a long-standing bug (present since the feature's initial commit, `1a8f983d`, with the identical unscoped-commits-since shape). The fix is straightforward: pass the already-computed `package_paths(pkg)` into the `commits_since` call (moving that computation above it) so the short-circuit is scoped identically to the fallback diff, using an empty scoped result to skip the diff (not a non-empty unscoped result to skip it).",
        "severity": "high",
        "verification_rationale": "Read crates/callisto-graph/src/changed.rs lines 51-67 directly: `git.commits_since(Some(&qualified), &[])` is called with an empty pathspec slice, and any non-empty result short-circuits `Ok(true)` before the path-scoped `git diff --quiet <tag> -- <paths>` fallback at lines 59-67 is ever reached. Verified in callisto-vcs (shell.rs:381-396 and access.rs:147-154) that an empty `pathspecs` slice causes no `--` filter to be appended, so `commits_since` runs an unscoped `git log <tag>..HEAD` across the entire repo, not the package's paths. Confirmed status.rs:86 calls this exact function to populate `changed_since_last_tag`, so the described downstream effect (near-always-true status field) is accurate. The finding is fully verified against the real code."
      },
      {
        "dimension": "bug",
        "title": "Fixed-group cascade convergence lacks the stale-member guard its Linked-group sibling just received",
        "primary_file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs",
        "primary_lines": "370-398",
        "other_locations": [
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs",
            "lines": "296-368 (the sibling Linked-group block that DOES have the guard)"
          },
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/aggregate.rs",
            "lines": "362-410 (union_fixed, which already guards against this exact stale-member case)"
          },
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs",
            "lines": "141-147 (the unguarded pkg_map.get(id).unwrap() that turns the unguarded insert into a panic)"
          }
        ],
        "description": "solve_cascade's Fixed-group convergence block (the `for g in input.groups.fixed.values()` loop, comment-labeled 'Track 1') is structurally a copy of the Linked-group block directly above it (lines 296-368), but it never got the stale-member guard that block was given in commit 08814168 ('fix(graph): fix stale-member crash risk in linked-group cascade convergence'). That commit's own message says the severity/target computation was 're-derived independently in four places' and that only aggregate's copies (union_fixed/union_linked) guarded against a group member removed from the workspace -- it then patched cascade's Linked-group copy but left the Fixed-group copy (right below it, same file) unpatched. Concretely: if callisto.toml's `[[group]]` (fixed) still lists a package that has since been removed from the workspace, and a live sibling in that group receives nonzero severity during cascade convergence, this loop unconditionally does `out.severities.insert(id.clone(), max_sev)` and `out.targets.insert(id.clone(), winner.clone())` for every `id` in `g.package_members()` -- including the stale one -- with no `input.base.contains_key(id)` check (contrast the Linked-group block's `if !input.base.contains_key(id) { ...push diagnostic...; continue; }`). The stale id then flows into `CascadeOutcome.severities`/`.targets`, and `plan_version` in commands/version.rs consumes it via `let pkg = pkg_map.get(id).copied().unwrap();` (line 147) -- `pkg_map` is built only from live graph packages, so this unwrap panics, crashing `callisto version` instead of emitting the same graceful UnknownPackage warning aggregate.rs's union_fixed already produces for the identical scenario.",
        "severity": "high",
        "verification_rationale": "Directly verified all four locations. cascade.rs lines 374-398 (Fixed-group \"Track 1\" loop) iterates `g.package_members()` and unconditionally calls `out.severities.insert`/`out.targets.insert` for every id with no `input.base.contains_key(id)` check, while the Linked-group block immediately above (296-368) explicitly filters into `live_members`, skips ids absent from `input.base`, and emits an `UnknownPackage` diagnostic. aggregate.rs's `union_fixed` (362-410) has the identical guard with a comment explicitly describing this exact stale-member crash scenario. commands/version.rs line 147 (`pkg_map.get(id).copied().unwrap()`) confirms the consequence: `pkg_map` is built only from live `ws.graph.packages()`, so a stale id flowing through from the unguarded cascade insert would panic. I also checked `groups::pre_mutation_checks` (groups.rs) and confirmed it does not reject or filter stale group members before cascade runs (it silently drops them via `filter_map` in one internal computation but performs no validation/error), so nothing upstream mitigates the gap. The bug is real and reachable exactly as described."
      },
      {
        "dimension": "duplication",
        "title": "Default tag template reimplemented via format!+parse instead of TagTemplate::default_for",
        "primary_file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/tags.rs",
        "primary_lines": "136-137",
        "other_locations": [
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-model/src/tag.rs",
            "lines": "72-77"
          },
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release.rs",
            "lines": "783-787"
          }
        ],
        "description": "`tag.rs` provides `TagTemplate::default_for(id)` as the single source of truth for a package's default tag template (`{display_name}@{version}`), and `release.rs`'s `unwrap_or_else(|| callisto_model::TagTemplate::default_for(&package.id))` correctly reuses it. But `tags.rs`'s per-package loop instead does `TagTemplate::parse(&format!(\"{}@{{version}}\", pkg.id.display_name()))?` -- hand-formatting the same string and re-deriving it through the full parse/validate path. Two independent implementations of 'what is this package's default tag template' that can silently diverge if `default_for`'s format ever changes (tags.rs would keep using the old convention). It's also a behavioral divergence, not just style: `default_for` builds the template directly with no git-ref-name legality check, while `tags.rs`'s reimplementation routes through `TagTemplate::parse`, which does validate ref-name legality and can `?`-propagate an error for a package name that `default_for`'s callers (e.g. `release.rs`) would silently accept as an unchecked template. Since this runs once per package in `TagIndex::build`'s workspace-wide loop, it's also redundant format!+parse+validate work per package that a plain call to the existing infallible helper would avoid.",
        "severity": "high",
        "verification_rationale": "Verified directly: tags.rs:138 builds the default template via `TagTemplate::parse(&format!(\"{}@{{version}}\", pkg.id.display_name()))?` inside TagIndex::build's per-package loop, while tag.rs:72-77 defines `TagTemplate::default_for(id)` constructing the same `{display_name}@` prefix directly (no validation), and release.rs:787 correctly calls `TagTemplate::default_for(&package.id)`. The two implementations are not just syntactically similar — they compute the identical domain value (a package's default tag template) via genuinely different code paths, and the behavioral divergence claim checks out: parse() calls is_valid_git_ref_name on the rendered test string (tag.rs:58-64) and returns/propagates an error on illegal names, whereas default_for() performs no such check, so tags.rs's loop can reject a package name that release.rs's default_for()-based path would silently accept unchecked."
      },
      {
        "dimension": "architecture",
        "title": "CRLF/format fingerprinting is ad hoc per-ecosystem, leaving Cargo.toml with no CRLF preservation at all",
        "primary_file": "crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs",
        "primary_lines": "13-23,204-216",
        "other_locations": [
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-manifests/src/npm.rs",
            "lines": "22-41,75-105,204-214"
          },
          {
            "file": "crates/callisto-manifests/src/python.rs",
            "lines": "18-27,39-45,66-75"
          }
        ],
        "description": "`CargoToml` tracks only `has_bom` and re-serializes via `toml_edit::DocumentMut::to_string()` in `persist()`, with no line-ending fingerprint at all. Meanwhile `PackageJson` (npm.rs) and `PyprojectToml` (python.rs) each independently invented an identical private `LineEnding { Lf, CrLf }` enum plus detect-on-open / reapply-on-persist logic (`content.contains(\"\\r\\n\")` then `.replace(\"\\r\\n\",\"\\n\").replace('\\n',\"\\r\\n\")`) -- byte-for-byte the same algorithm in two files. Because this concern was never lifted into a shared `common.rs` abstraction (the way `is_bare_semver`/`split_single_operator_prefix` were), it was applied inconsistently: Cargo, the third of the three canonical formats, never received it. The practical consequence is a real format-preservation regression that CLAUDE.md calls out as a named invariant (\"Format Preservation\"): any `Cargo.toml` authored/checked out with CRLF line endings (common on Windows or with `core.autocrlf=true`) will have every line ending silently rewritten to LF the moment `write_version`/`update_dependency_spec` + `persist` run, even though the equivalent `package.json`/`pyproject.toml` files in the same repo would have their CRLF preserved. There is also no CRLF fixture or test anywhere for Cargo.toml (`callisto_fixtures::corpus` only has CRLF samples for pyproject.toml), confirming the gap was never exercised.",
        "severity": "high",
        "verification_rationale": "Read cargo.rs (open/persist), npm.rs, and python.rs directly. CargoToml tracks only `has_bom` and its `persist()` (lines 205-216) does `self.document.to_string()` with zero CRLF handling. PackageJson and PyprojectToml each independently define an identical private `enum LineEnding { Lf, CrLf }` with byte-identical detect (`content.contains(\\\"\\\\r\\\\n\\\")`) and reapply (`.replace(\\\"\\\\r\\\\n\\\",\\\"\\\\n\\\").replace('\\\\n',\\\"\\\\r\\\\n\\\")`) logic. common.rs has no shared fingerprint/line-ending abstraction (only is_bare_semver/split_single_operator_prefix), confirming this was never lifted out, and there's no comment anywhere marking Cargo's omission as deliberate. python.rs has dedicated CRLF-preservation tests (lines 821, 869) with no Cargo.toml equivalent, matching the finding's claim about missing fixtures/tests."
      },
