{"type":"idle_notification","from":"adv-errprop-001","timestamp":"2026-09-09T22:48:29.120Z","idleReason":"available","result":"**Verdict: REFUTED** — ADV-ERRPROP-001 is not a defect. None of the four dropped `Result`s can leak an OS process or produce a misleading success.\n\nFull function: `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs:109-313` (`run_with_timeout_impl`). Lines 181/251 are in the two reader threads; 268/269 are the timeout-KILL branch.\n\n**1 & 2 — `drop(stdout_tx.send(text))` (181) / `drop(stderr_tx.send(text))` (251)**\n\n`mpsc::Sender::send` has exactly one failure mode: the receiver was dropped. `stdout_rx`/`stderr_rx` are locals of `run_with_timeout_impl`, so a failed send means the parent already returned — via the timeout branch (277-289), the `?` at 261-264, or the normal-exit path (299-312) whose `recv_timeout` expired. In every one of those cases the parent has already decided the output is unavailable and warned about it (280-284, 302-305) or is returning an error. The value being discarded is a `String` the thread owns; dropping it frees memory rather than leaking. Nothing observable is lost that the parent hadn't already given up on.\n\n**3 — `drop(child.kill())` (268)**\n\nReachable only from the `None` arm of `try_wait` (266), so `Child::status` is `None` on this iteration — which rules out std's only synthetic error (`InvalidInput`, \"can't kill an exited process\", raised when the child was already reaped). The remaining path is `libc::kill(pid, SIGKILL)`. `ESRCH` is unreachable because the child has never been waited on: even if it exits in the window between `try_wait` and `kill`, it is an unreaped zombie whose PID is still valid and still ours, and `SIGKILL` to a zombie succeeds as a no-op. `EPERM` cannot apply to our own child. There is no realistic error to propagate.\n\n**4 — `drop(child.wait())` (269)**\n\n`wait()` reaps. `EINTR` is retried inside std (`cvt_r`); `ECHILD` requires a prior reap, which cannot have happened (no earlier `wait`, and std installs no `SIGCHLD` handler). So the direct child is reaped and no zombie remains in this process. Post-`SIGKILL` the wait is also bounded — the signal is uncatchable.\n\n**Does anything leak?** No zombie: the direct child is signalled and reaped. Descendants that inherited the pipe fds do survive the kill — but that is an explicit, documented design decision (comments at 142-149, 254-257, 270-276) with two regression tests (`run_with_timeout_bounds_reader_join_when_descendant_holds_pipe_open`, `run_with_timeout_warns_on_timeout_branch_when_descendant_holds_pipe_open`), and orphaned grandchildren reparent to init and are reaped there — they are orphans, not zombies. **Misleading success?** No: `CommandError::TimedOut` is returned unconditionally at 286 regardless of what `kill`/`wait` returned, so no failure is dressed up as success. Grepping `TimedOut` across `crates/` shows no caller inferring \"process cleaned up\" from it — the only sites are the definition (`crates/callisto-model/src/exec.rs:126`), the construction, and a test assertion.\n\n**Adjacent observation, not this candidate (and not itself worth filing):** the `?` on `child.try_wait()` at `runner.rs:261-264` returns without killing or reaping, and `std::process::Child::drop` neither kills nor reaps — so that path would leak a live process plus a zombie. It's a `?` propagation rather than a dropped `Result`, and the trigger is unconstructible in practice (`waitpid(WNOHANG)` on our own never-reaped child has no reachable error: `ECHILD` needs a prior reap, `EINTR` is retried in std)."}
