{"type":"launched"}
{"type":"started","key":"v2:d509a094adc0a4c96f4df66cd9c5b5018be00d6d7b993639501e5bb3b245bc8c","agentId":"aa5d590ef6cc69839","label":"Enumerate every site in the Rust workspace at /Users/gabe/Pr","phase":"Recon"}
{"type":"result","key":"v2:d509a094adc0a4c96f4df66cd9c5b5018be00d6d7b993639501e5bb3b245bc8c","agentId":"aa5d590ef6cc69839","result":{"categories":[{"name":"Blanket ReleaseIntentStale sink","count":28,"shape":".map_err(|_error| GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale) or a catch-all `_ => Err(GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale)` arm, discarding a distinct upstream Err (rich ArtifactManifestError/RegistryError enums, CommitSha/JSON/Version parse errors, git command failures) and replacing it with one bare unit variant whose fixed help text says \"Regenerate and reapprove the release intent\" -- 24 confirmed .map_err sites plus 4 confirmed catch-all match sites in crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release.rs, release_decision.rs, and release_execution.rs (a separate ~70 bare `Err(GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale)` boolean-check sites in the same files are a related but distinct smell -- one error code for many validation conditions -- and are NOT included in this count because there is no discarded Err value at those sites).","representative_examples":[{"file":"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_execution.rs","line":46,"discarded_cause":"ArtifactManifestError -- a 5-variant thiserror enum (NonGitSource, DuplicateSlot, MismatchedSlotRoster, MismatchedAttestation, MismatchedIntent) returned by ArtifactManifestV1::validate_for_intent","folded_into":"GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale (unit variant, help text: \"Regenerate and reapprove the release intent; no release operation was authorized.\")","harm":"An operator whose CI built the wrong artifact slot roster, or whose attestation doesn't bind the release commit, sees generic 'stale intent' guidance and is told to re-approve the intent -- which does nothing, since the intent was fine and the artifact manifest was the actual problem. The purpose-built enum exists specifically to tell these five causes apart and is thrown away at the one call site that needs it most."},{"file":"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release.rs","line":358,"discarded_cause":"RegistryError::RateLimited(Duration) / AuthFailed(_) / Other(String) from classify_cargo_output / classify_npm_publish_output / classify_twine_output, which already parse registry CLI output specifically to distinguish these causes (with tests proving RateLimited carries the real retry-after duration)","folded_into":"GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale via `.map_err(|_error| GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale)?`","harm":"A publish that fails because npm rate-limited the request for 30s, or because the registry token is invalid, is reported identically to a stale-intent failure -- with remediation advice (\"regenerate and reapprove the release intent\") that is actively wrong for both real causes and will send an operator chasing the wrong fix during a live release."},{"file":"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_decision.rs","line":195,"discarded_cause":"serde_json::Error from parsing the committed ReleaseDecisionV1 JSON read back out of the release commit (which line/field was malformed)","folded_into":"GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale","harm":"A corrupted or hand-edited decision file blob in git history is indistinguishable from every other one of the ~15 distinct fail-closed checks in this same function (SHA malformed, changelog not updated, manifest version mismatch, unauthorized manifest change, etc.) -- all resolve to the same message, so an operator debugging a rejected release commit has no way to know which of ~15 checks actually tripped."},{"file":"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_decision.rs","line":139,"discarded_cause":"ModelError::InvalidCommitSha{raw, reason} from CommitSha::parse on `git rev-parse HEAD` output","folded_into":"GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale","harm":"Same sink as above: a malformed HEAD SHA (which should never happen and points at a real git-plumbing problem) is reported with the same message as an intentional staleness rejection, hiding a genuinely surprising failure behind routine-looking output."}]},{"name":"Parser rejection reasons discarded for user-supplied identifiers","count":3,"shape":".map_err(|_error| SomeError::Invalid{ raw }) discarding a multi-variant FromStr-style parse error (GitHubRepositoryParseError, PackageIdParseError) that already distinguishes the specific rejection reason, replacing it with a message that only restates the input string back at the user","representative_examples":[{"file":"crates/callisto-cli/src/commands/release_pr.rs","line":53,"discarded_cause":"GitHubRepositoryParseError::{MissingSeparator, TooManyParts, InvalidOwner, InvalidRepo} -- four textually distinct, differently-actionable parse failures","folded_into":"ReleasePrDecisionError::InvalidRepository { repository }","harm":"A user who typed `owner/repo/extra` (too many parts) gets the identical error as one who typed `owner#repo` (invalid characters) or `justonestring` (no separator) -- the message names only the bad string, not what specifically about it is wrong, even though the parser already knew."},{"file":"crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs","line":574,"discarded_cause":"PackageId::ParseError distinguishing a syntactically malformed identifier (leading slash/hyphen, path traversal, bad ecosystem prefix) from a well-formed identifier","folded_into":"GraphError::UnknownPackage { id }","harm":"This conflates two operationally different situations under one label: 'you typed an invalid package identifier' (fix: correct your syntax) vs. 'you typed a valid identifier but no such package exists in this workspace' (fix: check the package list) -- these call for different next steps and the collapsed message can't tell the operator which one applies."}]},{"name":"Version/grammar comparison mismatch loses the actual grammar pair","count":2,"shape":"Version::compare(...).map_err(|_grammar_mismatch| ...) reconstructs a new error without the GrammarMismatch{left, right} payload that compare() already produced","representative_examples":[{"file":"crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs","line":340,"discarded_cause":"GrammarMismatch{left, right} naming exactly which two VersionGrammar values collided (e.g. SemVer vs Pep440) during cascade group version comparison","folded_into":"GraphError::GroupGrammarMismatch{ group, members } -- richer in domain context (which cascade group, which members) but still drops which two grammars actually mismatched","harm":"Moderate: the cause space is narrow (there are only two grammars in this codebase), so an operator can usually infer the mismatch is SemVer-vs-PEP440 from the ecosystems involved, but the error itself no longer states it directly."}]},{"name":"workspace_relative() path-validation causes collapsed to one message","count":3,"shape":"callisto_model::workspace_relative(s).map_err(|_err| ConfigError::Invalid*{ .. } / LocateError::OutsideWorkspaceRoot{ .. }) discarding ModelError's AbsolutePath vs. NonUtf8Path vs. PathTraversal distinction","representative_examples":[{"file":"crates/callisto-graph/src/config/resolve.rs","line":260,"discarded_cause":"ModelError::{AbsolutePath, NonUtf8Path} -- two structurally different reasons a configured changelog path can be rejected","folded_into":"ConfigError::InvalidChangelogPath{ pattern, value }","harm":"An operator who configured an absolute changelog path gets the same message as one whose path contains non-UTF-8 bytes; the fix differs (drop the leading slash vs. re-encode the filename) but the error gives no hint which applies."}]},{"name":"moon CLI failure conflation (spawn failure vs. real nonzero exit)","count":2,"shape":"self.runner.run(\"moon\", [...]).map_err(|_err| LocateError::MoonUnavailable) immediately followed by a sibling `if out.exit_code != Some(0) { return Err(LocateError::MoonUnavailable) }` -- both routes discard the real cause into a variant whose own doc-comment admits it means two different things","representative_examples":[{"file":"crates/callisto-moon/src/locator.rs","line":36,"discarded_cause":"The actual process-spawn io::Error (binary not on PATH, permission denied, etc.) on the map_err branch, and moon's own stderr output entirely on the sibling nonzero-exit branch just below (project-graph command failed for a project-specific reason, e.g. a malformed moon.yml)","folded_into":"LocateError::MoonUnavailable (\"moon CLI is unavailable or exited non-zero\" -- the message itself documents the conflation)","harm":"An operator whose moon.yml is broken and whose `moon project-graph --json` therefore exits non-zero with a specific, actionable stderr message is told exactly the same thing as an operator who never installed moon at all -- the one piece of information (moon's own error output) that would tell them which situation they're in is discarded rather than attached."}]},{"name":"String-typed error wrapping without a #[source]/#[from] chain","count":2,"shape":"fs::create_dir_all(...).map_err(|_error| VcsError::Git(\"static message\".to_string())) -- VcsError::Git(String) has no #[source] field at all, so no site in the crate can preserve a structured chain; ~65 sibling call sites in the same crate at least interpolate the cause's Display text via format!(\"...: {e}\"), but these two do not even do that","representative_examples":[{"file":"crates/callisto-vcs/src/release_lock.rs","line":44,"discarded_cause":"std::io::Error from fs::create_dir_all (permission denied, read-only filesystem, disk full, invalid path -- all distinct, all implement std::error::Error) building the release-state directory","folded_into":"VcsError::Git(\"could not create release state directory\".to_string()) -- a fixed string with no interpolated cause and no #[source]","harm":"A release blocked because the state directory can't be created (e.g. a read-only CI filesystem, or a permissions problem) gives the operator no indication of *why* -- and this crate's own convention two lines and one file away (`format!(\"...: {e}\")`) shows preserving the io::Error's message would have cost nothing."}]}],"total_confirmed_sites":40,"systemic_fix_warranted":true,"systemic_fix_rationale":"The dominant category alone (28 of 40 confirmed sites) is a single crate collapsing dozens of textually and semantically distinct causes -- rich, purpose-built multi-variant enums (ArtifactManifestError, RegistryError with RateLimited/AuthFailed payloads), parse errors, and git-command failures -- into one bare unit variant (GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale) whose fixed help text is actively misleading for most of the real causes it now stands in for. That is not scattered carelessness; it is a load-bearing convention in callisto-graph's release path (the single most safety-critical code in the workspace, per its own doc comments about \"no release operation was authorized\"), and per-site patches would leave the next release-path bug hidden behind the same sink. The second and sixth categories (parser-rejection folding, and VcsError::Git(String) lacking any #[source] field) are structural too: PackageIdParseError and GitHubRepositoryParseError already do the work of distinguishing causes and it is thrown away at the one or two call sites that surface it to a human, and VcsError::Git's shape makes a real source chain impossible even for call sites that want one -- 65 of 67 constructions work around this by manually interpolating {e} into a String, which is itself evidence the type should carry a #[source] instead. A workspace-wide fix is warranted: (1) give GraphError::ReleaseIntentStale a `reason: ReleaseIntentStaleReason` field (or split it into a handful of #[source]-carrying variants) so the ~28 sites above compile only if they pass the real cause through; (2) add a #[source] field to VcsError::Git (or a `Git { message: String, #[source] source: Option<...> }` shape) so the crate's own {e}-interpolation convention becomes structural instead of a manual habit; (3) add a workspace clippy/CI check that flags `.map_err(|_` and bare unit-variant catch-alls in non-test code, since the existing 40 sites show the compiler currently has zero opinion about a closure parameter nobody reads. Patching the 40 call sites individually without also constraining the error *types* they map into would leave the underlying shape (a variant or wrapper that structurally cannot hold a cause) free to accumulate the same defect at every future call site.","worst_crates":["callisto-graph","callisto-vcs","callisto-moon"]}}
